(2) digits of the constant Gamma(P)
    5, 3, 4, 9, 2, 8, 7, 9, 3, 2, 0, 2, 2, 6, 5, 7
    -> "Sorry, but the terms do not match anything in the table."
       Advanced matches at the level of the digit set only.

(3) number of strict local minima, first k odd primes, target floor(T/2)
    1, 2, 2, 2, 6, 10, 14, 22, 44, 66, 113, 214, 353, 628, 1190, 2138, 3831, 6706,
    12339, 22808
    -> "Sorry, but the terms do not match anything in the table.  There were no advanced
        matches found."

(4) number of ground states, same instances
    1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2, 3, 4, 8, 14, 22, 38, 70, 126, 218, 394, 708, 1290, 2354, 4344
    -> "Sorry, but the terms do not match anything in the table.  There were no advanced
        matches found."


reading
-------
The prediction made when the queries were written -- that (4) was the likeliest to be
already catalogued, being the least exotic object -- was wrong.  (4) is not there either:
the number of near-equal splittings of the first k odd primes is not in OEIS.

What this establishes and what it does not.  It is absence of evidence over the searches
made, at a database of a stated size on a stated date, and OEIS grows.  It is not a proof
of novelty and the paper does not say it is; the status label on the sentence in Part I
says "literature pass, not a proof of novelty".  What it removes is the weaker and now
false sentence that stood there before -- that the sequences had not been looked up in a
way we could point at.  They have, and this is the pointer.
